:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the central structure right. It says Irish UCITS ETFs need not publish full holdings daily. It names daily public holdings as one route. It treats periodic disclosure as conditional on daily arbitrage information for APs and market makers, not as a blanket exemption, which matches the grader note.\\n\\nIt omits several substantive elements:\\n- **Transparency-policy disclosures:** it lists the prospectus and website. It does not mention the KID/KIID or marketing materials, or disclosure of where portfolio and iNAV information is available. It also wrongly frames the policy as being about information given to APs.\\n- **Periodic-route conditions (CBI Q&A 1012):** it leaves out the core conditions:\\n  - the arbitrage information must be available on a nondiscriminatory basis;\\n  - the prospectus must disclose that information;\\n  - there must be procedures for when arbitrage is impaired;\\n  - there must be procedures for investor information requests;\\n  - quarter-end holdings must be published within 30 business days.\\n- **Holdings and iNAV detail:** it does not say that holdings disclosure identifies positions and quantities, or that the prospectus must explain iNAV calculation and frequency.\\n- **Common requirement:** it does not say that both routes keep the transparency-policy disclosures.\\n\\nThe answer contains no outright contradiction, but its coverage of the conditions is too generic to grade as correct.\"}","ttft_ms":2492,"type":"result","duration_ms":6901,"uuid":"891d76f5-638f-4533-9692-bb7164645a82","ttft_stream_ms":537,"time_to_request_ms":71,"first_content_frame_ms":537,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
